      "content": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    \n    // Read matrix using scanf for speed (as in original)\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> mat(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i][j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Build prefix sum matrix (integral image)\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> ps(n + 1, std::vector<long long>(n + 1, 0));\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n            ps[i][j] = mat[i-1][j-1] + ps[i-1][j] + ps[i][j-1] - ps[i-1][j-1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    int mxSum = INT_MIN;\n    const int limit = n - s;  // inclusive upper bound for starting indices\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum)\n    for (int r = 0; r <= limit; ++r) {\n        for (int c = 0; c <= limit; ++c) {\n            long long sum = ps[r + s][c + s] - ps[r][c + s] - ps[r + s][c] + ps[r][c];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = static_cast<int>(sum);\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "path": "src/solution.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Parallelize submatrix maximum sum using integral image and OpenMP parallel for with reduction to achieve work sharing and fine-grained parallelism.",
  "notes": "The integral image approach eliminates sequential dependencies, enabling full parallelism. Collapse clause improves load balance across 4 cores.",
